solutionMd:
## The idea

Members are nodes in a graph and connections are edges, so "degrees of connection" is the **shortest path in an unweighted graph** — breadth-first search (BFS). BFS explores everyone 1 hop away, then 2 hops, and so on, so the first time we see the target we know the distance is minimal. For the follow-up, search from **both ends** at once and always grow the smaller side; when the two searches touch, add their depths.

## Step by step

1. If either member is null → -1. Same member → 0.
2. BFS from the start: a queue of members to visit and a set of member ids already seen (so cycles cannot loop forever).
3. Process one whole level at a time and count levels. When a neighbour is the target, return the current level.
4. If the queue runs out, the target is in a different part of the network → -1.
5. Bidirectional: keep a frontier and a seen-set for each side. Each round, expand only the smaller frontier by one level. If a newly reached member is already seen by the other side, the searches met: return the number of levels expanded in total.

## Worked example

**Input:** bob — alice — frank;  Distance(bob, frank)

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| Start | queue = [bob], seen = {bob} |
| Level 1 | bob's connections: alice (not frank) → queue = [alice] |
| Level 2 | alice's connections: bob (seen), frank = target → return 2 |

**Result:** 2

## Cost

BFS: O(V + E) time and O(V) memory for the members reached. Bidirectional search reaches far fewer members when one side is a hub with thousands of connections, because it always expands the smaller side.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// IMember is provided (Models.cs): GetMemberId(), GetConnections().
public static class ConnectionDistance {

    // Plain BFS from one side. Distance is counted in hops.
    public static int Distance(IMember from, IMember to) {
        if (from == null || to == null) return -1;
        int targetId = to.GetMemberId();
        if (from.GetMemberId() == targetId) return 0;

        HashSet<int> seen = new HashSet<int>();
        seen.Add(from.GetMemberId());
        Queue<IMember> queue = new Queue<IMember>();
        queue.Enqueue(from);
        int depth = 0;

        while (queue.Count > 0) {
            depth++;                               // everyone found in this pass is `depth` hops away
            int levelSize = queue.Count;
            for (int i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) {
                IMember current = queue.Dequeue();
                IList<IMember> connections = current.GetConnections();
                if (connections == null) continue;

                foreach (IMember next in connections) {
                    if (next == null) continue;
                    int id = next.GetMemberId();
                    if (id == targetId) return depth;
                    if (!seen.Contains(id)) {      // never visit anyone twice
                        seen.Add(id);
                        queue.Enqueue(next);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return -1;                                  // not connected at all
    }

    // Search from both ends; always expand the smaller frontier.
    public static int DistanceBidirectional(IMember from, IMember to) {
        if (from == null || to == null) return -1;
        if (from.GetMemberId() == to.GetMemberId()) return 0;

        List<IMember> frontA = new List<IMember> { from };
        List<IMember> frontB = new List<IMember> { to };
        HashSet<int> seenA = new HashSet<int> { from.GetMemberId() };
        HashSet<int> seenB = new HashSet<int> { to.GetMemberId() };
        int depth = 0;

        while (frontA.Count > 0 && frontB.Count > 0) {
            depth++;
            bool met;
            if (frontA.Count <= frontB.Count) {
                frontA = ExpandOneLevel(frontA, seenA, seenB, out met);
            } else {
                frontB = ExpandOneLevel(frontB, seenB, seenA, out met);
            }
            if (met) return depth;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    // Visit every neighbour of the frontier. `met` becomes true if we touch the other search.
    private static List<IMember> ExpandOneLevel(List<IMember> frontier, HashSet<int> mySeen,
                                                HashSet<int> otherSeen, out bool met) {
        List<IMember> next = new List<IMember>();
        foreach (IMember current in frontier) {
            IList<IMember> connections = current.GetConnections();
            if (connections == null) continue;
            foreach (IMember neighbour in connections) {
                if (neighbour == null) continue;
                int id = neighbour.GetMemberId();
                if (otherSeen.Contains(id)) { met = true; return next; }
                if (!mySeen.Contains(id)) {
                    mySeen.Add(id);
                    next.Add(neighbour);
                }
            }
        }
        met = false;
        return next;
    }
}
```

